WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.950
Azure's DNS-layer filter
is DNS resolver policy.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.112
Microsoft renamed it from DNS security
policy, so expect both names.

3
00:00:10.112 --> 00:00:15.346
A resolver policy filters and logs
every query in a linked virtual network,

4
00:00:15.346 --> 00:00:18.976
public and private, with
allow, block and alert rules.

5
00:00:18.976 --> 00:00:22.014
It includes a Microsoft-managed
threat intelligence

6
00:00:22.014 --> 00:00:24.850
list from the Microsoft
Security Response Center.

7
00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:28.946
Its logs go to Log Analytics,
in the DNS query logs table,

8
00:00:28.946 --> 00:00:33.225
or to storage or Event Hubs,
through diagnostic settings.

9
00:00:33.225 --> 00:00:37.118
Microsoft lists resource logs
only for resolver policies,

10
00:00:37.118 --> 00:00:41.487
so the inference is that per-query
logs come from a resolver policy,

11
00:00:41.487 --> 00:00:45.300
or Azure Firewall's DNS proxy,
not from the resolver itself.

12
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.744
DNSSEC: Azure can sign public zones,

13
00:00:47.744 --> 00:00:51.200
but the Azure-provided
resolver doesn't validate.

14
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.466
No Microsoft page describes
signing private zones,

15
00:00:54.466 --> 00:00:58.250
or whether the Private Resolver
validates, so confirm that.
